==Old English==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|gem-pro|ang}} {{recons|þurbanan|lang=gem-pro}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|ang}} {{recons|terp-|lang=ine-pro}}.  Germanic cognates include Old Frisian *{{term||thurva}}, Old Saxon ''[[thurvan]]'' (Dutch ''[[durven]]''), Old Norse ''[[þurfa]]'' (Swedish ''[[tarva]]''), Old High German ''[[thurfan]]'' (German ''[[dürfen]]''), Gothic *{{term||𐌸𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌱𐌰}}.  The Indo-European root also led to Greek {{Grek|[[τερπειν]]}}, Old Slavic ''[[trěbě]]'' (Russian {{Cyrl|[[требоватъ]]}}).

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈðurvɑn/|lang=ang}}

===Verb===
{{ang-verb|type=pretpres}}

# to [[need]], to be [[obliged]] (to do something)

